Z||000007||1FieroGTRacingInc||10-05-2000||11:06 PM||||Just thought of this.....  awile back I had the dist. replaced ....    could my dist. be installed wrong...  I meanmaybe I need to take the dist. out and turn it a few teeth or so??   what do you all think?  steve||||reg||1
Z||000008||mrfiero||10-05-2000||11:36 PM||||Check your timing with a timing light before you start removing the distributor.  Factory spec is 10* BTDC.  You can loosen the distributor and turn it either way to advance or retard the timing.  If you can't get it to the proper spec then you will need to remove the distributor and move it a tooth.<P>That aside.....my GT seems to idle at 500 RPM in drive (sometimes down to 350!).  I'm not sure if the gauge is reading wrong or ??  I just reset the timing, but it's still low.<P>I also owned an '87 coupe that seemed perfectly happy idling at 400 RPM (in drive).<P> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||BroCar 87||10-05-2000||11:40 PM||||How did you replace your distributer? 87's are DIS ignition aren't they. Coil packs, crank sensors, prom... that kinda stuff. Didn't all 87's have this or is there 87's that had the distributer??? I am lost.||||reg||4
Z||000010||Phil||10-06-2000||07:02 AM||||He has a GT   V6s have dist.||||reg||1
Z||000011||1FieroGTRacingInc||10-06-2000||08:46 AM||||Orge...  would removing mt dist. and turning it a tooth or so(advance it)...  would it be worth a shot to try that to see if that might work.....   Everytime I stop for the last 30,000 miles I have had to put it in (N). and I'm tired of doing that and besides that it cannot be good on my tranny to keep shifting it like that...  Thank's   Steve||||reg||1
Z||000012||william_thorniii||10-06-2000||09:09 AM||||maybe Mr. Ogre would respond if you didn't just call him orge... too close to orgy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Seriously though follow Mikes advice and try to set the timing by just rotating the dist.  If you can't get it where you need it to be, then you can try to remove the dist and jump a tooth.  <P>There are several sensors that can cause a similar problem such as the Idle Air Control, the Manifold Air Pressure and the Oxygen Sensor.  It is quite possible that your timing is quite alright, but you are not getting enough fuel or air.  I would pull your diagnostic codes from your ECM first.  Then second would listen to the car's idle... usually I time by ear for rough guestimates anyway.  <P>Hope this helps,<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||3
Z||000013||william_thorniii||10-06-2000||09:11 AM||||Oh yeah... I forgot... Ogre is the master of the DIS Dukes.  He doesn't get involved in v6 discussions too often.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000014||Eric_PA||10-06-2000||09:44 AM||||I had the same problem with my automatic. It's an 86.5 GT. It would stall when I would come to a stop. I had to keep my foot on the gas and brake. I found that the EGR valve was bad and was not setting any codes. I would recomend checking that.||||reg||1
Z||000015||theogre||10-06-2000||10:03 AM||||Daaammmmmm people slow down.... I don't spend all my time online. It just seems that way. :P <P>It's doing this with the TC unplugged?<P>Rotating the distributor shaft won't matter. You still have to set the thing to spec and that's controlled by the body. After setting the timing check these.<P>1. check MAP and it's vac line carfully. That hard plastic line likes to crack or block up. <P>2. make sure there are no vac leaks and that everything is plugged to the right ports. the routing is on the VECI label. check the EGR hardware.<P>3. make sure the MAT sensor is ok and not full of trash. dirt on that thing can skew it readings. It's not quite as big a deal for the V6 as it is in the DIS 4 but it still can throw off the fuel mix.<P>The ecm tries to idle as low as it posibly can. The floating will change under differant conditions. it does it more when the engine is cold. <P>An 87 V6 should have the new style weather packed coolant sensor. If it's got the old style round connector replace it. Borg Warner includes the new connector and instructions with the sensor. follow those instructions. (Yes, the connector wire Must be Soldered. Crimps alone won't do.)<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||1FieroGTRacingInc||10-06-2000||10:22 PM||||Thank's to everybody for the responses...   Thank you also MR.ORGE. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">   Steve||||reg||1
Z||000017||BAM-BAM||10-07-2000||01:48 PM||||     Could it be torque converter lock up?<P> Just a thought <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P> Later BAM OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Don B. <BR>85GT<BR>Eibach Springs/KYB shocks and struts<BR>Mr.Mikes 500 series seats, Carpet and headliner! YEAH BABY-YEAH||||reg||12
